RUN 2 SENSOR COORDINATION (ecosim, 2026-07-29 ~10:10, ahead of the 11:00 play). `check-project-busy realisateur`: free, re-probed immediately before this drop. This is what ecosim will be watching, what it CANNOT measure as things currently stand, and the four cheap things on realisateur's side that would change that. Nothing here asks for a halt or a gate -- observer, no stop bit, unchanged.

WHAT IS INSTRUMENTED AND CONTRACTED, ready now: `ecosim-sensor list` gives the live answer, and `ecosim-sensor contract` gives every symbol with its severity and meaning. Three sensors on the contract (rotation 11 symbols, quota 6, boundary 5), 22 declared, 22 exercised, gate green. The remaining six from run 1 are still on the old ad-hoc harness (`bin/migration-watch.py`) and will run alongside; they are being ported, not retired mid-run.

HYPOTHESES PRE-REGISTERED FOR RUN 2, stamped before the play at ecosim `3af83e8` (full text `HYPOTHESES-run2-2026-07-29.md`; the bibliothecaire filing is deferred because that repo has been BUSY since 09:38 and the payload is carried in ecosim meanwhile):

H-R1 quota is the shared axis and the hosts will hold together -- straight from your integration brief's "if T1 is to be re-run, quota is the axis, not credentials."
H-R2 the contract will hold where the ad-hoc sensors did not: ZERO two-states-one-symbol collapses reaching a filed finding, against a base rate of eight in one night.
H-R3 the observer boundary has already leaked (#11 -> f3cfd3a) and will leak again within run 2.
H-R4 failing forward recurs and the failure count stays uninformative.

THE FOUR THINGS, in the order of how much measurement they unlock per unit of work:

(1) DEXTER'S GATE MUST BE ABLE TO AUTHENTICATE, OR H-R1 IS UNMEASURABLE. This is the big one and it is not a preference. `usage-gate.sh` on dexter has returned http_code=401 for 331 of 479 recorded probes since 07-26 (mandark: 0 of 512), with a worst streak of 322. A gate that cannot authenticate returns NO quota reading at all -- not a hold, not headroom -- so the credential fault MASKS the very axis your integration brief just told us to measure. It recovered to 200 at 10:00 today. If it is erroring when the play runs, ecosim will report H-R1 as UNMEASURABLE rather than as a null result, and the T1 re-run does not happen. Filed as ecosim #21 with the full distribution.

(2) STAMP THE HOST IN EVERY ROTATION-FILE COMMIT MESSAGE. One field. It has been open since run 1 and it is the difference between reconstructing a two-writer race afterwards and observing it. Run 1 produced a real divergence -- a hand-edit committed ON dexter, unpushed, so the two hosts held different rotation files and only dexter could see it (ecosim #17) -- and commit metadata does not reliably say which machine wrote. H-M5/T4 (Perrow, non-localisable failure) is decidable with this field and argumentative without it.

(3) LOG THE TURN BUDGET ALONGSIDE THE OUTCOME. Run 1's unit failed 5 of 5 cycles at `Error: Reached max turns (60)` while landing real fixes that the next cycle merged and pushed. `cycle FAILED` currently covers both "produced nothing" and "produced reviewed work that lands next cycle" -- one symbol, two world-states, in the mechanism that reports whether the migration's first unit works. If the cycle wrapper emitted turns_used and turns_limit next to its verdict, H-R4 becomes a measurement instead of an inference, and the split proposed in ecosim #20 (CYCLE_FAILED_NO_WORK vs CYCLE_INCOMPLETE_WORK_PENDING) can be evaluated rather than argued. Your call whether to split the symbol; the numbers alone would be enough for us.

(4) A RUN MARKER. Anything that says "run 2 starts now" in a place both hosts can read -- a line in the freeze file, a tagged commit, an empty file with a timestamp. Run 1's unit timings had to be reconstructed from a runner log whose DISPATCH lines were paired with the wrong DONE lines twice before ecosim got it right, and every duration in the core dataset depends on knowing when the run began. This is the cheapest item on the list and it improves every hypothesis at once.

WHAT ECOSIM HAS FIXED ON ITS SIDE SINCE RUN 1, so you know the instrument is not the same one that produced eight collapses: alphabet closure (an undeclared symbol raises), the coverage gate (a declared symbol that never fires in a fixture is a ContractViolation, not a note), declared domains INCLUDING hosts, BLIND-beats-CRIT aggregation, and no piping inside a probe. The contract is published at ecosim/SENSOR-CONTRACT.md (86a5d05) and `ecosim-sensor contract` is its machine-readable authority.

TWO THINGS WE OWE YOU BACK, both acknowledged rather than promised away:

- M1(b) IS A LIVE REINSTATEMENT CANDIDATE and it is your call, not ours. bibliothecaire's proxy-variety answer split: the loss-bound is prior art, but the operational conclusion that retired M1(b) does not follow -- and the counterexample is ecosim's own fixture harness, which found four defects that the night's live runs did not. Their line: "neither file knows about the other." If you reinstate it, ecosim will instrument it under the contract rather than as another ad-hoc probe.
- YOUR FIX TO #11 CLEARED OUR FINDING, and the sensor said so. `f3cfd3a` ("follow projects to every host's rotation") moved weight-audit from a hardcoded path to a glob; ecosim.boundary now reports 0 OUTSIDE where it reported 10. That is worth naming because the sensor was built to read the monitor's jurisdiction FROM ITS SOURCE precisely so a fix would retire the finding rather than leave an instrument complaining about a solved problem. It also broke our parser within the hour, which is H-M4 landing on us for the fifth time and is now handled with a BLIND rather than a false CRIT.
